-- title:
--   Cusps of the special fibre lie on one component only
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$ and a nonzero natural number $M$ with $p \mid M$ and $p^2 \nmid M$, a subgroup $H \le (\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$ containing every unit whose image under reduction $(\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/(M/p))^\times$ is $1$, with $M/p$ nonzero, and assume the $q$-series $j$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ lies in the intermediate field of $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}(\mathbb{Q})$ generated by the integral form ratios at level $\mathrm{SL}(2,\mathbb{Z})$. Let $\mathfrak{X}$ be a model datum of type `XHDRModelAtP p M H hpM hj`, let $A$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $p$ in its nonunits, whose residue field $\kappa$ is algebraically closed of characteristic $p$, and let $\rho : R p \to A$ be a ring homomorphism compatible with the structure map $R p \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$. Let $i \in \{0,1\}$ and let $P_0$ be a closed point of the curve underlying the fibre curve model $\mathfrak{X}.\mathrm{Mfib}$, and assume the associated place $v = \mathrm{placeOfPoint}(P_0)$ is not affine, i.e. there are no $x$ in the function field `Fbar p M H hpM κ` whose Laurent expansion is the $j$-series over $\kappa$ and no $a \in \kappa$ with $v$ taking the value $a$ at $x$. Then for every $j \in \{0,1\}$ with $j \neq i$, the image of $P_0$ under $\mathfrak{X}.\mathrm{efib}$ followed by the $i$-th component map $\mathfrak{X}.\mathrm{comp}\,i$ does not lie in the range of the underlying map of $\mathfrak{X}.\mathrm{comp}\,j$.
--
--   On the Deligne–Rapoport model at a prime exactly dividing the level, the special fibre is the union of two components crossing at the supersingular points; the statement says that a point whose place is a cusp (the $j$-series has a pole there, so no finite value is taken) is carried into only one of the two components. It is used in the analysis of the cusps of the special fibre, namely in the local semicontinuity statement for the prolongation datum and in the identification of the infinity-side and zero-side reductions of a cusp.
-- source:
